      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <img
    class="page-header-img"
    src="/images/headers/cmi-command-verbs-explained.webp"
    alt="CMI command verbs explained — from Identify to Critically Appraise"
    width="1376"
    height="688"
    loading="eager"
    decoding="async"
  />

  <TableOfContents />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="what-are-command-verbs">What Are CMI Command Verbs and Why Do They Matter?</h2>
      <p>
        Each assessment criterion in a CMI unit begins with a command verb, such as "Explain the role of
        leadership" or "Critically appraise the impact of organisational context". The verb sets the type and
        depth of response needed. CMI states that learners must provide evidence that they have met all of the
        criteria and should ensure that their responses fully address the command verbs used within each one.
      </p>
      <p>
        CMI qualifications are graded Pass or Refer, and every criterion has to be met. A response that
        describes when the criterion says evaluate, or that stops before the judgement the verb requires,
        leaves that criterion unmet however accurate the content is. Your centre sets the task and its assessor
        decides whether the verb has been met, so use this guide alongside your brief.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="verb-hierarchy">How the Verbs Group — Describe, Analyse and Judge, Critique and Build</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">CMI's verbs ask for different kinds of work. The groups below follow the definitions in Annex 1 and the verbs each level actually uses.</p>
      <div class="tier-grid">
        <div class="tier-card">
          <h3 class="tier-name">Show Knowledge</h3>
          <p class="tier-levels">Most common at Levels 3 and 4</p>
          <p class="tier-verbs">Identify &middot; Define &middot; Describe &middot; Outline &middot; Explain &middot; Summarise</p>
          <p class="tier-desc">State, describe and make clear what something is and how it works. Explain is the most frequent verb at Level 3, with 112 of its 257 criteria.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-card tier-card--mid">
          <h3 class="tier-name">Analyse and Judge</h3>
          <p class="tier-levels">Levels 4 and 5, and still present at 6 and 7</p>
          <p class="tier-verbs">Discuss &middot; Analyse &middot; Examine &middot; Assess &middot; Evaluate &middot; Justify</p>
          <p class="tier-desc">Break a subject down, weigh evidence and reach a reasoned judgement. Analyse, Examine and Evaluate are 165 of the 248 criteria at Level 5.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-card tier-card--high">
          <h3 class="tier-name">Critique and Build</h3>
          <p class="tier-levels">Levels 6 and 7</p>
          <p class="tier-verbs">Critically appraise &middot; Critically assess &middot; Critically evaluate &middot; Develop &middot; Propose &middot; Recommend</p>
          <p class="tier-desc">Question the strength of the evidence and the validity of the sources, then produce a strategy, proposal or recommendation. 29 of 75 criteria at Level 6 and 37 of 80 at Level 7 begin with Critically.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-h3">The Verbs Each Level Uses Most</h3>
      <div class="plan-grid">
        <div class="plan-card"><h3>Level 3 (257 criteria)</h3><p>Explain 112, Identify 38, Outline 33, Discuss 27, Summarise 17, Assess 10, Compare 9.</p></div>
        <div class="plan-card"><h3>Level 4 (85 criteria)</h3><p>Describe 16, Discuss 12, Identify 11, Explain 10, Evaluate 5, Analyse 5.</p></div>
        <div class="plan-card"><h3>Level 5 (248 criteria)</h3><p>Analyse 66, Examine 51, Evaluate 48, Assess 22, Discuss 21, Develop 10, Recommend 10.</p></div>
        <div class="plan-card"><h3>Level 6 (75 criteria)</h3><p>Critically appraise 12, Critically assess 9, Discuss 8, Recommend 7, Evaluate 5, Develop 5.</p></div>
        <div class="plan-card"><h3>Level 7 (80 criteria)</h3><p>Critically appraise 18, Discuss 9, Develop 8, Critically assess 7, Propose 5, Recommend 5.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Counts are from the current CMI syllabuses for each level (Level 3 October 2024, Level 4 July 2026,
        Level 5 June 2026, Level 6 July 2026, Level 7 August 2026). Your unit may use others, so always follow the
        verb on your own criteria.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="identify">Identify <span class="level-tag">Mainly Level 3 and 4</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Identify as ascertaining the origin, nature or definitive characteristics of something. It sits beside Define (show or state clearly and accurately) and Outline (a broad account that gives the essential features but not the detail).</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> name the items precisely and, where the criterion says so, say what makes each one what it is. Identify is the second most common verb at Level 3, with 38 criteria.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="describe">Describe <span class="level-tag">Mainly Level 3 and 4</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Describe as providing an extended range of detailed factual information about the topic or item in a logical way.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> give an accurate, organised account of the features. Describe is the most common verb at Level 4, with 16 of 85 criteria. If the criterion says Explain, a description alone does not meet it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="explain">Explain <span class="level-tag">Mainly Level 3 and 4</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Explain as making something clear to someone by describing or revealing relevant information in more detail.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> go beyond what something is to show how or why. Explain is the dominant verb at Level 3, with 112 of 257 criteria, and appears in 10 Level 4 criteria.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="discuss">Discuss <span class="level-tag">Levels 3 to 7</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Discuss as giving a detailed account that includes a range of views or opinions, including contrasting perspectives. Critically discuss adds an evaluation of the pros and cons and of the effects and impacts of the topic.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> present more than one view and show how they relate, rather than a single account. Discuss appears at every level: 27 criteria at Level 3, 12 at Level 4, 21 at Level 5, 8 at Level 6 and 9 at Level 7.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="analyse">Analyse <span class="level-tag">Mainly Level 4 and 5</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Analyse as breaking the subject or complex situation into separate parts and examining each part in detail, identifying the main issues and showing how the main ideas relate to practice and why they are important. Reference to current research or theory may support the analysis.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> use a theory or model as a tool to examine your own situation, rather than describing the theory. Analyse is the most common verb at Level 5, with 66 of 248 criteria. Unit 514 uses it for the environmental and internal factors that drive change.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="examine">Examine <span class="level-tag">Mainly Level 5</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Examine as inspecting something thoroughly in order to determine its nature or condition.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> look closely enough to say what the thing is and what it means for the organisation. Examine is the second most common verb at Level 5, with 51 criteria.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="evaluate">Evaluate <span class="level-tag">Mainly Level 5</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Evaluate as considering the strengths and weaknesses, arguments for and against, and similarities and differences. The writer should then judge the evidence from different perspectives and make a valid conclusion or reasoned judgement, applying current research or theory where it supports the evaluation.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> weigh the evidence and then decide. A list of strengths and weaknesses with no stated conclusion does not meet the verb. Evaluate has 48 criteria at Level 5, for example Unit 514 AC2.1, evaluate the use of theoretical models for managing change. A response that only describes Kotter and Lewin does not meet it. One that weighs them against each other for the same change, and judges which better explains what happened, does.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="justify">Justify <span class="level-tag">Level 5</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Justify as providing a rationale for actions or decisions, underpinned by research, academic theory, data analysis or experience.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> state the decision and then give the reasons for it. Justify is used sparingly, in 3 of the 248 criteria at Level 5.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="assess">Assess <span class="level-tag">Levels 3 to 5</span></h2>
      <p>CMI defines Assess as providing a reasoned judgement or rationale of the standard, quality, value or importance of something, informed by relevant facts. Appraise is close: assessing and estimating the worth, value, quality or performance of something and considering carefully to form an opinion.</p>
      <p class="worked-note"><strong>In practice:</strong> give your judgement and the facts behind it. Assess has 22 criteria at Level 5 and 10 at Level 3.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="critically-evaluate">The Critically Verbs <span class="level-tag level-tag--primary">Levels 6 and 7</span></h2>
      <p>
        Levels 6 and 7 are where "Critically" appears. CMI defines each one as the base verb done critically. In every
        case it adds that critical work considers the strength of the evidence, based on the validity of the method
        used to compile it, and that sources must be checked for validity.
      </p>
      <div class="ce-steps">
        <div class="ce-step"><span class="ce-num">1</span><p><strong>Critically appraise</strong>: a systematic process to identify the strengths and weaknesses of information in order to assess its usefulness and validity.</p></div>
        <div class="ce-step"><span class="ce-num">2</span><p><strong>Critically assess</strong>: as with assess, but emphasising judgements made about arguments by others, and about what is being assessed from a different perspective. A reasoned argument based on judgements.</p></div>
        <div class="ce-step"><span class="ce-num">3</span><p><strong>Critically evaluate</strong>: as with evaluate, considering strengths and weaknesses and arguments for and against, then judging the evidence from different perspectives and reaching a valid conclusion.</p></div>
        <div class="ce-step"><span class="ce-num">4</span><p><strong>Critically reflect</strong>: as with reflect, identifying, questioning and assessing deeply held beliefs and assumptions, including those about your own practice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="worked-note">
        <strong>Counts:</strong> Critically appraise is the most common verb at both levels, with 12 criteria at Level 6 and 18 at
        Level 7. Critically assess has 9 at Level 6 and 7 at Level 7, and Critically reflect 3 and 4. Critically evaluate appears
        once at Level 6 and 4 times at Level 7. Critically discuss, Critically examine, Critically review and Critique make up most
        of the rest. <strong>Example:</strong> Unit 701 AC1.1 asks you to critically appraise the impact of organisational
        context on strategic leadership, so judge how context helps and hinders strategic leadership, and check the validity of
        the sources you use to do so. See <a href="https://www.cmiassignmenthelp.uk.com/cmi-level-6-assignment-help/">CMI Level 6 assignment help</a>
        and <a href="https://www.cmiassignmenthelp.uk.com/cmi-level-7-assignment-help/">CMI Level 7 assignment help</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="critically-analyse">What About Critically Analyse? <span class="level-tag">Defined, not used</span></h2>
      <p>
        CMI defines Critically Analyse in Annex 1 as analysis that questions and tests the strength of a person's or
        others' analyses from different perspectives, using analysis to make an objective and reasoned argument. Even so,
        no assessment criterion in the Level 3 to Level 7 syllabuses begins with it. At Level 7, for example, the critical
        verbs actually used are Critically appraise, Critically assess, Critically reflect, Critically evaluate,
        Critically review, Critically discuss, Critically examine and Critique. If a brief or a third-party guide tells
        you a level is marked on a particular verb, check it against the criteria in your own unit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow verb-section">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="develop-a-plan-strategy">Develop, Propose and Recommend <span class="level-tag">Levels 5 to 7</span></h2>
      <p>These verbs ask you to produce something rather than judge something.</p>
      <div class="plan-grid">
        <div class="plan-card">
          <h3>Develop</h3>
          <p>CMI defines Develop as elaborating, expanding or progressing an idea from a starting point, building on the information given. It has 10 criteria at Level 5, 5 at Level 6 and 8 at Level 7. Unit 514 asks you to develop a plan for change, and Unit 704 an organisational strategy to achieve a business objective.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="plan-card">
          <h3>Propose and Recommend</h3>
          <p>CMI defines Recommend as putting forward proposals, alternatives or suggestions supported by a clear rationale appropriate to the situation. Recommend has 10 criteria at Level 5, 7 at Level 6 and 5 at Level 7. Propose appears in 5 Level 7 criteria, such as Unit 708's Risk Management Framework.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="worked-note">Whatever you build, link it back to your analysis: a plan, strategy or recommendation with no evidence behind it is the weakest part of most submissions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="grade-mapping">Command Verbs and Pass or Refer</h2>
      <p>
        CMI qualifications are graded Pass or Refer. There is no Merit or Distinction. A Pass means every
        assessment criterion in the unit has been met, and meeting a criterion means doing what its verb asks. A Refer
        means at least one criterion is not yet met. If you have been referred, see our
        <a href="https://www.cmiassignmenthelp.uk.com/guides/cmi-resubmission-guide/">resubmission guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="assessors-guide">Checking Your Answer Against the Verb</h2>
      <p>
        Your assessor marks each criterion against its verb. Before you submit, check each answer against these four
        questions:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Which verb does the criterion actually use?</strong> Check every criterion separately, because verbs differ between criteria in the same unit.</li>
        <li><strong>Is the operation complete?</strong> An Evaluate answer needs strengths and weaknesses, a judgement of the evidence and a conclusion. Stopping before the conclusion leaves the criterion unmet.</li>
        <li><strong>Have you checked your sources?</strong> At Levels 6 and 7, the Critically verbs ask you to consider the validity of the sources you use.</li>
        <li><strong>Are your conclusions and recommendations drawn from your analysis?</strong> CMI asks for appropriate use of evidence, examples and references to support them.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Your centre's assessment brief and its assessor have the final say on what counts as a pass in your unit.
        CMI publishes its full definitions in Annex 1 of each syllabus.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="verbs-faq">CMI Command Verbs — Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-q">What is the difference between Evaluate and Critically Evaluate in CMI assignments?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>CMI defines Evaluate as considering the strengths and weaknesses, arguments for and against, and similarities and differences, then judging the evidence and reaching a valid conclusion or reasoned judgement. Critically Evaluate is the same, but critically: it also considers the strength of the evidence based on the validity of the method used to compile it. Critically Evaluate appears in 4 of the 80 Level 7 criteria and once at Level 6, while Evaluate is one of the most common verbs at Level 5.</p></div></details>
        <details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-q">What does Analyse mean in a CMI assignment?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>CMI defines Analyse as breaking the subject or complex situation into separate parts and examining each part in detail, identifying the main issues and showing how the main ideas relate to practice and why they are important. Reference to current research or theory may support the analysis. It is the most common verb at Level 5, with 66 of the 248 criteria.</p></div></details>
        <details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-q">Is Critically Analyse used in CMI assessment criteria?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>CMI defines Critically Analyse in Annex 1, but none of the assessment criteria in the Level 3 to Level 7 syllabuses begins with it. At Level 6 and Level 7 the critical verbs actually used are Critically appraise, Critically assess, Critically evaluate, Critically examine, Critically reflect, Critically discuss, Critically review and Critique. Always follow the verb on your own criteria.</p></div></details>
        <details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-q">Why did I get a Refer if I used the right command verb?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>A Refer means at least one assessment criterion is not yet met. A response can attempt a verb without completing it, for example evaluating strengths and weaknesses but never reaching a reasoned judgement, or it can miss part of the criterion. Compare your answer with the exact wording of each criterion and read your assessor's feedback. Our resubmission guide explains the next steps.</p></div></details>
        <details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-q">Do all CMI units use the same command verbs?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Verbs are set by criterion. Level 3 relies mostly on Explain, Identify, Outline and Discuss. Level 4 uses Describe, Discuss, Identify, Explain, Evaluate and Analyse. Level 5 uses mostly Analyse, Examine and Evaluate. Levels 6 and 7 use the Critically verbs heavily, with Develop, Propose and Recommend for build tasks. Identify every verb in every criterion of your brief before writing.</p></div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

